00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:58.930
Now what am I going
to talk about today

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:03.220
is first, I'm going to basically
step back a couple of years

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:06.370
and give a brief history of
how we came up with Spanner

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:10.060
and why, and then explain
what Cloud Spanner is,

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:11.320
in particular.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:13.180
I will show you
some demos and how

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:15.970
you can get started as well.

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:19.340
So what is Spanner?

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:22.850
Going back about 12
years-- like 2005--

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:27.640
and Google's business was on the
back of a sharded MySQL right?

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:31.290
So every dollar that we made
with advertising on our AdWords

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:34.510
system was backed by a
sharded MySQL system that

00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:36.880
was sharded by a customer ID--

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:40.030
and then occasionally, we
had to reshard this system

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:43.600
to be able to grow even
further and further.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:47.640
Now the last reshard that
we did took several years,

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:48.570
for a matter of fact.

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:51.190
And at that point,
we were saying, OK,

00:01:51.190 --> 00:01:52.700
we can't do that any longer.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:55.480
We-- like, our business
can't grow at the pace

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:58.630
that we are growing and
keeping all the qualities

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:04.970
with being on back of a sharded
traditional database system.

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:07.180
So that's why we
came up with Spanner.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:08.889
We needed to build
a system which

00:02:08.889 --> 00:02:12.900
has a lot of the qualities of
a traditional database system,

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:16.450
but has on top of this things
like horizontal scaling.

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:18.460
So we had a lot of customers.

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:21.400
We were growing very
quickly from a $10 million

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:26.860
business a year to a billion
dollar business a year,

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:29.440
and we were dealing
with people's money.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:31.270
We had customers.

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:32.950
We had budgets that
they could set.

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:36.070
We had partners, agencies,
and things like that.

00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:38.470
We couldn't work
with a system that

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:41.110
relies on eventual
consistency because this

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:46.570
would mean that we could lose
data and we could lose money.

00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:52.510
Also, this system, Google,
is a 24/7, 365 business.

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:57.340
It's operating around the world,
and we cannot deal with any

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:58.090
kind of downtime.

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:00.265
Every time we are
down with our system--

00:03:00.265 --> 00:03:02.530
with our backend systems--
it basically means

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:06.880
a lot of millions or thousands
of dollars that we are losing,

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:09.020
and our customers as well.

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:13.000
So prior to Spanner,
there was no such system

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:16.785
that where we basically could do
updates of our database system

00:03:16.785 --> 00:03:18.160
with no downtime--
where we could

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:21.040
do schema changes
with no downtimes,

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:22.960
and things like that.

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:26.650
Now how does that
relate to Cloud Spanner?

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:30.040
Let's step 10 years
later, roughly.

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:32.770
And what we do
with Cloud Spanner

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:34.720
is basically making
this internal system

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:36.340
that we build for--

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:38.770
which our business relies on.

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:41.200
We make this available
to all of you.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:44.560
So that means you have the same
performance and scalability

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:48.410
and consistency that we have
with our internal Spanner.

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:51.160
It's the same team
that builds it,

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:54.790
plus the team that builds the
integration with our Cloud

00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:55.870
things.

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:58.870
And it's the same team
that keeps the reliability

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:00.430
of this system--

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:01.840
the system that
we use internally

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:04.000
as well as the
system that you get

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:06.880
to use on the Cloud platform.

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:09.240
So what was really
important here

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:12.370
was that this is
one system which

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:14.470
goes into the direction
of a cloud native systems.

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:16.390
So it's a fully managed system.

00:04:16.390 --> 00:04:19.820
You basically can do with click,
set up your Spanner instance.

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:22.780
And you can scale
it transparently.

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:24.310
And also, what was
really important

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:26.120
when we built the
system internally

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:28.600
is people understood the--

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:31.000
to work with a
MySQL or PostgreSQL

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:34.510
relational database system
which has ACID transaction.

00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:36.940
And it's much harder to
deal with a system which

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:41.470
has only low-level consistency
or eventual consistency.

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:44.490
Writing apps on top of that--
especially financial apps--

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:46.790
is really hard because you
have to build, basically,

00:04:46.790 --> 00:04:48.730
your transactional
layer on top of this.

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:52.930
So we needed a system which
has the traditional semantics

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:55.400
of a relational database system.

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:59.600
So Spanner comes with these
traditional semantics as well.

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:02.055
We also wanted to integrate
with existing tools

00:05:02.055 --> 00:05:02.930
and things like that.

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:06.820
So you can basically undust
your SQL skills and work

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:11.560
with Spanner like with
the familiar SQL language.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:15.250
Now a really important
thing for us as well is--

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:19.620
I mentioned no downtime
and 24/7 business.

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:22.270
We really needed a
really reliable system.

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:26.270
We needed a system where we
can have high availability.

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:28.450
So if any hardware
failure is there,

00:05:28.450 --> 00:05:30.790
that the database
doesn't go down.

00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:34.390
And that we do by having
a synchronous replication

00:05:34.390 --> 00:05:36.400
off the data in
Spanner and having

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:37.750
high reliability with this.

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:40.510
And I go in a little bit more
detail what that actually

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:42.110
means.

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:46.160
And what you also get with
putting your applications

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:48.470
on Cloud Spanner
is a system which

00:05:48.470 --> 00:05:50.330
has been battle
tested within Google.

00:05:50.330 --> 00:05:54.620
Our AdWords system relies
from the start on it.

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:58.760
But also, nowadays, a lot more
of the internal and external

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:01.460
apps that we have rely,
actually, on top of Spanner.

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:04.310
So for matter of fact, if you
look at Google Cloud platform,

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:07.580
the control plane, which
basically tracks everything

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:10.040
when you started GCE
instances and things

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:15.710
like that-- the control plane is
based on top of Cloud Spanner.

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:18.200
Now what Google is
really committed to

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:20.600
is being the open cloud.

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:28.010
So we try really hard to have
as least log in as possible.

00:06:28.010 --> 00:06:32.630
So we try to integrate
open standards,

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:36.050
build a multitude
of client libraries

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:38.200
so that you can integrate
with your applications

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:39.980
that you already have existing.

00:06:39.980 --> 00:06:43.340
So we have client libraries
and job [INAUDIBLE],,

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:46.070
and there are more
languages coming up.

00:06:46.070 --> 00:06:50.990
We also have the GoPC
stop as open source

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:53.940
so you can build your own
languages-- own client

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:55.920
stops as well [INAUDIBLE].

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:59.210
We also provide currently
read-only gdpc driver

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:01.910
to integrate with tools like
business intelligence tools

00:07:01.910 --> 00:07:03.431
and things like that.

00:07:03.431 --> 00:07:05.180
What was really important
for us, as well,

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:08.180
for a cloud-based solution
is, from the start,

00:07:08.180 --> 00:07:10.070
to integrate Cloud
Spanner with things

00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:13.550
like audit locking,
identity and access

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:18.210
management, and encryption
on rest of your data.

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:21.480
Also, if you look
at the SQL language

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:24.200
standards that is used
within Google Cloud Platform,

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:26.980
it wasn't always that we
had a coherent story there.

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:30.150
So we put, actually, a lot of
effort in in the recent months

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:33.600
and years to standardize
on the SQL dialect

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:36.120
that we are using
throughout our products.

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:40.220
And Cloud Spanner
uses, also, SQL NZ 2011

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:43.230
as, for instance, Big Query
is using, with some extensions

00:07:43.230 --> 00:07:45.630
on top of this.

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:50.070
Now we get asked a lot,
like, how does Cloud Spanner

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:53.070
compare to a traditional
relational database

00:07:53.070 --> 00:07:55.620
system, or a
non-relational database

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:58.836
system-- like a so-called
NoSQL the database?

00:07:58.836 --> 00:08:00.210
Now, if you compare
Cloud Spanner

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:03.620
to a traditional
relational database system,

00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:06.780
you basically look at schema,
SQL, and consistencies.

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:08.640
And both of these
systems are like

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:10.230
a traditional
relational database,

00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:12.360
and Spanner provide this.

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:15.340
Where they divert
is availability.

00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:18.180
Now you can build a
high-available system

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:20.350
with a traditional
database system.

00:08:20.350 --> 00:08:26.640
But usually, what that means
is you create a read replica.

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:28.200
You create a failover.

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:30.120
You have to manage all these.

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:32.070
And basically, with
a read replica,

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:37.830
you free up your master node a
little bit of the read workload

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:40.770
to get more performance
towards the right workload.

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:42.909
But your traditional
relational database

00:08:42.909 --> 00:08:47.170
is only vertical
scaling database.

00:08:47.170 --> 00:08:50.930
Now what it also means,
if you have a failover

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:54.200
instance or a read replica
in a sharded system,

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:55.760
you need to manage all these.

00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:58.400
You need to manage
updates of the systems.

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:01.280
You might have to failover
your master to your failover

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:04.130
instance when you want
to take down your master

00:09:04.130 --> 00:09:07.790
and update the hardware or
update the US, which basically

00:09:07.790 --> 00:09:11.500
means even if it is planned,
you incur a downtime.

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:13.280
But this is not the
case with Spanner.

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:17.390
We don't have
downtime with Spanner.

00:09:17.390 --> 00:09:22.070
The other thing is if you look
at scalability, as mentioned,

00:09:22.070 --> 00:09:23.900
in a traditional
database system,

00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:26.360
you basically have
vertical scalability, too--

00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:31.700
and you can, to some extent,
go higher and higher instance,

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:34.460
like if you wait til your cloud
provider provides a bigger

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:34.991
machine.

00:09:34.991 --> 00:09:37.490
And you hope that they provide
a bigger machine while you're

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:39.050
reaching the ceiling.

00:09:39.050 --> 00:09:41.060
And you can do things
like read replicas

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:43.640
to free your master
from this read workload,

00:09:43.640 --> 00:09:47.840
but you still have a ceiling
in terms of scalability.

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:50.600
If you compare Cloud Spanner
to a non-relational database

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:54.110
system, you already
see in the beginning

00:09:54.110 --> 00:09:58.130
most of the NoSQL systems
don't have a rigid schema.

00:09:58.130 --> 00:10:00.950
They don't have
necessarily SQL support

00:10:00.950 --> 00:10:04.647
It might come with an
addition or some of them

00:10:04.647 --> 00:10:07.580
partly provide an
SQL, but it's not

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:11.510
like you are used to it from
a MySQL PostgreSQL system.

00:10:11.510 --> 00:10:14.040
And what's really important
is the consistency.

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:16.520
Now you have in certain
NoSQL database systems

00:10:16.520 --> 00:10:22.390
the possibility to basically
tweak your consistency model.

00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:23.820
But it comes with trade-offs.

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:27.650
It comes with trade-offs of
scalability and performance

00:10:27.650 --> 00:10:29.010
of your system.

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:32.000
Now you don't need to make these
trade-offs with Cloud Spanner.

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:38.180
It's really performance out
of the box for read and write.

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:39.850
So what are the
typical workloads

00:10:39.850 --> 00:10:43.840
that we see with Spanner
that we have seen in our EAP?

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:47.660
And now since Spanner
is beta and GA--

00:10:47.660 --> 00:10:50.190
currently is actually
[INAUDIBLE] GA--

00:10:50.190 --> 00:10:52.150
we've seen a multitude
of applications

00:10:52.150 --> 00:10:53.830
that are moving to Spanner.

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:57.040
And I want to take out three
that are really important,

00:10:57.040 --> 00:11:00.580
and which I think
where the Spanner has

00:11:00.580 --> 00:11:02.010
really the strengths.

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:04.960
So I mentioned of scalability.

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:08.660
Now if you look at a system
in terms of scalability,

00:11:08.660 --> 00:11:10.990
for your write and
your read scalability,

00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:13.630
that's where Spanner
is really strong.

00:11:13.630 --> 00:11:15.460
You can horizontal scale.

00:11:15.460 --> 00:11:19.462
And you basically scale your
write and read very seamlessly.

00:11:19.462 --> 00:11:20.920
So that's one thing
where you don't

00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:25.760
have these limits that you
have on a traditional system.

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:28.210
Another really important
thing is with Spanner,

00:11:28.210 --> 00:11:30.430
even though it's a
horizontal scalable system,

00:11:30.430 --> 00:11:31.930
you have transactional.

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:34.600
You have asset, and you
have strong consistency.

00:11:34.600 --> 00:11:37.720
Actually, we're really adamant
about this in the sense

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:40.230
that we have external
serializability

00:11:40.230 --> 00:11:44.860
as our consistency
model in Spanner.

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:50.620
Now especially if you look at
a financial fin tag or things

00:11:50.620 --> 00:11:52.450
like that, this is
really important

00:11:52.450 --> 00:11:56.020
that you have these capabilities
and that transactions can

00:11:56.020 --> 00:11:58.110
span rows and multiple tables.

00:11:58.110 --> 00:12:02.390
You're not limited to a
certain part of your database.

00:12:02.390 --> 00:12:06.130
What is also a really
strong use case for Spanner

00:12:06.130 --> 00:12:09.240
is a global data plane where
basically Spanner comes in

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:11.350
as a highly available system.

00:12:11.350 --> 00:12:14.470
Spanner is by design a
highly available system.

00:12:14.470 --> 00:12:17.320
If you get a regional
set up, it comes up

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:24.110
with four nines of availability,
which is like four minutes

00:12:24.110 --> 00:12:27.010
38 seconds of downtime a month.

00:12:27.010 --> 00:12:29.830
Now if you use a
multi-regional set-up, which

00:12:29.830 --> 00:12:32.560
comes later this
year, you go down

00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:35.680
to 26 seconds of
downtime a month,

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:41.190
which translates to five
nines of availability SLA.

00:12:41.190 --> 00:12:44.670
Now how does Spanner
actually achieve that?

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:48.160
How does Spanner
look under the hood?

00:12:48.160 --> 00:12:51.960
So if you look at Spanner,
if you buy one node from us,

00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:55.330
you basically get a node
which is replicated in three

00:12:55.330 --> 00:12:57.432
zones in a regional instance.

00:12:57.432 --> 00:12:58.890
So you have your
regional instance,

00:12:58.890 --> 00:13:02.070
and you have your
replication of your compute

00:13:02.070 --> 00:13:04.260
and your storage in three zones.

00:13:04.260 --> 00:13:07.770
Now what's really important
here is that storage and compute

00:13:07.770 --> 00:13:10.320
are separated in a
way that they are

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:11.530
independent from each other.

00:13:11.530 --> 00:13:14.010
So you can scale
compute and storage

00:13:14.010 --> 00:13:16.740
independent from each other.

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:18.810
So what does it mean?

00:13:18.810 --> 00:13:21.210
What are all these terms?

00:13:21.210 --> 00:13:23.760
If you look at Spanner,
you start with an instance.

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:25.530
That's something
that you basically,

00:13:25.530 --> 00:13:28.320
when you create your
Spanner database,

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:29.820
you start with an
instance where you

00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:33.960
define which region do I want
to have my Spanner instance in?

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:38.140
And how many nodes do I want to
have for my Spanner instance?

00:13:38.140 --> 00:13:41.910
Now in the Spanner instance, you
can create up to 100 databases.

00:13:41.910 --> 00:13:44.820
And in this database you
can create a multitude--

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:46.470
up to 200--

00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:49.470
hundreds of tables.

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:51.210
Now these are the
concepts that are

00:13:51.210 --> 00:13:53.710
used to form a traditional
database system.

00:13:53.710 --> 00:13:57.060
And these are the concepts
that are visible to you.

00:13:57.060 --> 00:14:00.300
Now behind the
scenes, we actually

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:03.956
split these tables
in so-called splits,

00:14:03.956 --> 00:14:05.800
or in contiguous key ranges.

00:14:05.800 --> 00:14:09.180
So based on your primary key, we
basically choose contiguous key

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:11.700
ranges and split your table.

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:14.790
That enables us to do the
horizontal scalability

00:14:14.790 --> 00:14:18.010
for write and read.

00:14:18.010 --> 00:14:20.995
So if you look,
you basically have

00:14:20.995 --> 00:14:26.410
your splits that are replicated
three times in each zone.

00:14:26.410 --> 00:14:27.580
You have your nodes.

00:14:27.580 --> 00:14:31.040
And for this example right
now, we look at only one node.

00:14:31.040 --> 00:14:32.380
So you buy one node from us.

00:14:32.380 --> 00:14:35.390
This is replicated
in three zones.

00:14:35.390 --> 00:14:42.890
Now what is really important is
that to be strong consistent,

00:14:42.890 --> 00:14:45.860
you really have to make sure
that only one is writing

00:14:45.860 --> 00:14:50.000
basically to a certain
split, and it's

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:52.130
in control of that data piece.

00:14:52.130 --> 00:14:56.270
Now what we use in Spanner is
the so-called Paxos algorithm,

00:14:56.270 --> 00:15:01.140
which is a majority voting
to vote leader in that group.

00:15:01.140 --> 00:15:04.850
So we have basically Paxos
groups spans all three zones.

00:15:04.850 --> 00:15:07.260
And they are basically
based on the split.

00:15:07.260 --> 00:15:10.700
So you have for
each replica, you

00:15:10.700 --> 00:15:13.620
choose one leader
in your Paxos group.

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:17.360
Now if you know and you come
from a distributative role,

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:20.570
you know making sure that
you actually have only one

00:15:20.570 --> 00:15:23.330
leader at any point
in time, it requires

00:15:23.330 --> 00:15:25.220
a lot of communication.

00:15:25.220 --> 00:15:27.230
Now communication is expensive.

00:15:27.230 --> 00:15:30.260
And that would basically
mean that you slow down

00:15:30.260 --> 00:15:32.680
your database.

00:15:32.680 --> 00:15:34.800
So what Google did is
they invented actually

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:38.790
a system which removes
the need of a lot

00:15:38.790 --> 00:15:40.170
of these communication.

00:15:40.170 --> 00:15:42.520
And that system is
called TrueTime.

00:15:42.520 --> 00:15:44.500
So what is TrueTime?

00:15:44.500 --> 00:15:48.000
So if you compare your
watches with your neighbor,

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:51.360
and you go out and you
come back in five minutes,

00:15:51.360 --> 00:15:54.450
if you have really detailed
watches in milliseconds,

00:15:54.450 --> 00:15:57.500
your watches will have
drifted from each other.

00:15:57.500 --> 00:16:00.730
The reason is atmospheric rays.

00:16:00.730 --> 00:16:02.010
It is temperature.

00:16:02.010 --> 00:16:04.000
It's pressure, all
kinds of things

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:08.350
that interfere with how fast
your clocks are running.

00:16:08.350 --> 00:16:13.050
So keeping clocks in sync across
oceans and across data centers

00:16:13.050 --> 00:16:16.250
is a really, really,
really hard problem.

00:16:16.250 --> 00:16:18.020
Now what Google
did is they build

00:16:18.020 --> 00:16:23.540
a system based on or consisting
of atomic clocks and GPS

00:16:23.540 --> 00:16:28.980
synchronization to synchronize
the clocks in all data centers.

00:16:28.980 --> 00:16:30.800
Now it's not a wall
clock time that you

00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:32.730
get actually one timestamp.

00:16:32.730 --> 00:16:35.570
What is really smart here
is actually you get a range.

00:16:35.570 --> 00:16:38.540
You get kind of like
a range of time.

00:16:38.540 --> 00:16:41.090
And what is this range?

00:16:41.090 --> 00:16:45.260
So you get a lower bound and
an upper bound timestamp.

00:16:45.260 --> 00:16:49.430
The lower bound timestamp means
that this time has definitely

00:16:49.430 --> 00:16:51.950
passed everywhere in the world.

00:16:51.950 --> 00:16:55.340
The upper bound timestamp
means that this timestamp

00:16:55.340 --> 00:16:58.780
has definitely not passed
everywhere in the world.

00:16:58.780 --> 00:17:01.200
And by the technology
that we have used,

00:17:01.200 --> 00:17:05.180
we can basically get
this interval down

00:17:05.180 --> 00:17:07.569
into a couple of milliseconds.

00:17:07.569 --> 00:17:09.510
And by having this
global wall clock time,

00:17:09.510 --> 00:17:15.180
we can make sure that we have
exactly one leader for a split

00:17:15.180 --> 00:17:16.349
at any point in time.

00:17:16.349 --> 00:17:18.329
And by that, we can
make sure that we

00:17:18.329 --> 00:17:20.849
have strong consistency
in the transactions

00:17:20.849 --> 00:17:25.540
that we are running in Spanner.

00:17:25.540 --> 00:17:28.710
Now let's show a
little bit how that

00:17:28.710 --> 00:17:32.280
looks like when you look
actually at a query that

00:17:32.280 --> 00:17:34.330
runs against Cloud Spanner.

00:17:34.330 --> 00:17:38.560
So in this case, I want
to do a consistent read.

00:17:38.560 --> 00:17:40.690
It basically means I
really want to have

00:17:40.690 --> 00:17:42.880
the latest data at that point.

00:17:42.880 --> 00:17:46.180
So what I start with is
my client sends a request.

00:17:46.180 --> 00:17:48.310
And it goes into
a front end, which

00:17:48.310 --> 00:17:50.710
figures out where
the responsible split

00:17:50.710 --> 00:17:51.730
for this query is.

00:17:51.730 --> 00:17:55.720
So there's a transparent
routing basically happening.

00:17:55.720 --> 00:18:02.670
Now in this case, we are routed
to a slave, or a replica,

00:18:02.670 --> 00:18:04.147
which is closest to us.

00:18:04.147 --> 00:18:06.605
And that replica basically
says, OK, this is a strong read.

00:18:06.605 --> 00:18:09.660
So I need to check if
I have the latest data.

00:18:09.660 --> 00:18:13.460
So it will do a request to
the leader of that split.

00:18:13.460 --> 00:18:16.040
And it will get back, yeah,
you have the latest data.

00:18:16.040 --> 00:18:19.730
And then we basically can
serve that data from the slave,

00:18:19.730 --> 00:18:21.660
from the read replica.

00:18:21.660 --> 00:18:26.530
Now in the case that you have
actually not the latest data--

00:18:26.530 --> 00:18:30.150
so we ask basically the leader,
do I have the latest data?

00:18:30.150 --> 00:18:32.466
The leader will
respond back, no, you

00:18:32.466 --> 00:18:33.590
don't have the latest data.

00:18:33.590 --> 00:18:36.400
But just wait a couple
of milliseconds,

00:18:36.400 --> 00:18:37.380
and you will have it.

00:18:37.380 --> 00:18:39.090
So it's not that
with this request

00:18:39.090 --> 00:18:40.770
we are sending
actually the data.

00:18:40.770 --> 00:18:43.350
Most likely at the point
that we are asking the leader

00:18:43.350 --> 00:18:44.990
if we have the
latest data, the data

00:18:44.990 --> 00:18:47.250
is already in flight
to the replicas

00:18:47.250 --> 00:18:51.670
so that we will basically
be in sync at a later point.

00:18:51.670 --> 00:18:54.480
So what basically happens
is the slave will wait out

00:18:54.480 --> 00:18:58.020
that time till it's up
to date to the timestamp

00:18:58.020 --> 00:18:59.640
when the request came in.

00:18:59.640 --> 00:19:02.040
And then once the
time is waited out,

00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:06.070
we can respond with the data.

00:19:06.070 --> 00:19:12.520
Now the thing is, there might be
use cases where you don't want

00:19:12.520 --> 00:19:16.570
to stress your leaders basically
with these calls to check

00:19:16.570 --> 00:19:18.250
if I have the latest data.

00:19:18.250 --> 00:19:22.760
There might be use cases
where you can read stale data,

00:19:22.760 --> 00:19:24.370
where you're OK to
read stale data.

00:19:24.370 --> 00:19:27.360
And especially if you look
at a multi-regional set up

00:19:27.360 --> 00:19:29.650
where you have
replication globally,

00:19:29.650 --> 00:19:32.410
this might be a good thing
to actually answer really

00:19:32.410 --> 00:19:34.660
quickly to your clients.

00:19:34.660 --> 00:19:36.580
Now how can you do this?

00:19:36.580 --> 00:19:38.650
The way to do this is
through so-called use

00:19:38.650 --> 00:19:42.650
stale queries where you give
a time bound of how much

00:19:42.650 --> 00:19:44.290
staleness you allow.

00:19:44.290 --> 00:19:46.880
So in this case I
basically say, all right,

00:19:46.880 --> 00:19:51.640
I want to have data which is
at maximum 15 seconds old.

00:19:51.640 --> 00:19:55.560
Now this comes in again through
one of our read replicas.

00:19:55.560 --> 00:19:59.080
And the read replica checks
basically timestamp of the data

00:19:59.080 --> 00:20:01.780
that it has for this
data that is requested

00:20:01.780 --> 00:20:04.800
and checks against the
timestamp bound and sees,

00:20:04.800 --> 00:20:08.140
OK, I have data
which is definitely

00:20:08.140 --> 00:20:10.030
newer than the timestamp bound.

00:20:10.030 --> 00:20:12.630
So I can return and
respond directly

00:20:12.630 --> 00:20:16.610
before without involving
the leader at all.

00:20:16.610 --> 00:20:19.490
Now the next concept is
read-write transaction.

00:20:19.490 --> 00:20:22.250
And just what I've
mentioned before,

00:20:22.250 --> 00:20:25.280
if you saw it the read-only
transaction that I

00:20:25.280 --> 00:20:28.540
talked in the beginning, it
didn't have any logs that way.

00:20:28.540 --> 00:20:30.260
So read-only
transactions can actually

00:20:30.260 --> 00:20:32.860
work without setting any
logs and slowing down

00:20:32.860 --> 00:20:35.270
my read-write transactions.

00:20:35.270 --> 00:20:37.940
Now in a read-write
transaction, what I basically

00:20:37.940 --> 00:20:41.660
do is when the request comes
in through my front end that

00:20:41.660 --> 00:20:43.850
does the routing, the
routing is directly

00:20:43.850 --> 00:20:46.220
done to the leader
of my Paxos group.

00:20:46.220 --> 00:20:49.910
Now the leader acquires
logs and sends back

00:20:49.910 --> 00:20:52.070
the query results
for the read that I'm

00:20:52.070 --> 00:20:55.040
doing in my transaction.

00:20:55.040 --> 00:20:56.490
Now then I'm doing something.

00:20:56.490 --> 00:20:57.650
I do a calculation.

00:20:57.650 --> 00:20:59.900
I'm adding some money to
it or things like that.

00:20:59.900 --> 00:21:02.390
And I'm sending back basically
the write that I want to do,

00:21:02.390 --> 00:21:04.550
the update of the data.

00:21:04.550 --> 00:21:07.760
Now in that case, we have
the logs already acquired.

00:21:07.760 --> 00:21:12.200
What we do is we're sending
the write to our read replicas.

00:21:12.200 --> 00:21:14.210
And as soon as a
majority comes back

00:21:14.210 --> 00:21:16.370
from this read replica,
which includes myself,

00:21:16.370 --> 00:21:20.090
the leader, at that point
I can basically say,

00:21:20.090 --> 00:21:22.940
OK, the data is going
to be committed.

00:21:22.940 --> 00:21:27.140
So I can respond
already to my client

00:21:27.140 --> 00:21:31.370
and say, OK, I can respond
and release the logs.

00:21:31.370 --> 00:21:35.000
And any replica that hasn't
responded up to that point

00:21:35.000 --> 00:21:36.170
basically will catch up.

00:21:39.590 --> 00:21:43.220
So I mentioned that
Spanner is based

00:21:43.220 --> 00:21:46.850
on the semantics
that you are used

00:21:46.850 --> 00:21:50.520
to from a relational
database system, be it MySQL,

00:21:50.520 --> 00:21:54.170
be it PostgreSQL, or any of
the major database vendors.

00:21:54.170 --> 00:21:57.380
Now you have very
familiar constructs.

00:21:57.380 --> 00:22:02.660
You have tables with columns
which have the standard SQL

00:22:02.660 --> 00:22:03.510
data types.

00:22:03.510 --> 00:22:05.030
So it's strongly typed.

00:22:05.030 --> 00:22:07.860
And what you can do
here, for instance,

00:22:07.860 --> 00:22:10.340
you have a singers table
and a albums table where

00:22:10.340 --> 00:22:12.980
you can define primary keys.

00:22:12.980 --> 00:22:14.000
Now there is one thing.

00:22:14.000 --> 00:22:17.720
You don't have foreign key
constraints in Spanner.

00:22:17.720 --> 00:22:19.400
But you have a
different concept,

00:22:19.400 --> 00:22:22.430
which is kind of like a foreign
key constraint minus minus,

00:22:22.430 --> 00:22:24.830
which we call
interleaved tables.

00:22:24.830 --> 00:22:27.020
Now what are interleave tables?

00:22:27.020 --> 00:22:29.875
I was just talking about Spanner
being distributed system,

00:22:29.875 --> 00:22:31.790
a horizontally
distributed system.

00:22:31.790 --> 00:22:34.700
And any time you need
to do any communication

00:22:34.700 --> 00:22:38.360
to get your data together to
do a response, that basically

00:22:38.360 --> 00:22:42.260
means time more spent
for actually getting

00:22:42.260 --> 00:22:44.580
your query results
to your clients.

00:22:44.580 --> 00:22:46.920
So if you have
certain queries where

00:22:46.920 --> 00:22:49.910
you want to co-locate the
data, where you basically you

00:22:49.910 --> 00:22:52.070
want to do some kind
of a [INAUDIBLE],,

00:22:52.070 --> 00:22:54.500
you can use the construct
of interleave tables, which

00:22:54.500 --> 00:22:58.540
basically ensures the
co-location of that data.

00:22:58.540 --> 00:23:01.360
And very straightforward
to do this.

00:23:01.360 --> 00:23:03.820
You basically do the
singers table here,

00:23:03.820 --> 00:23:06.180
and then the album
table undefined.

00:23:06.180 --> 00:23:09.910
And the album table is
an interleaved table

00:23:09.910 --> 00:23:13.290
of your singers table.

00:23:13.290 --> 00:23:18.570
Now there are certain schema
things that you can do.

00:23:18.570 --> 00:23:21.560
And there are certain things
that you should not do.

00:23:21.560 --> 00:23:24.270
So like anti-patterns
that are used

00:23:24.270 --> 00:23:27.690
to do in a vertical
scalable database system

00:23:27.690 --> 00:23:31.540
are here actually hurting
if you move to Spanner.

00:23:31.540 --> 00:23:34.080
So I want to point
out a couple of those.

00:23:34.080 --> 00:23:39.240
One is I mention that
Spanner tables are split up

00:23:39.240 --> 00:23:41.580
by contiguous key ranges.

00:23:41.580 --> 00:23:44.970
Now you can imagine if you
use a monotonic in increasing

00:23:44.970 --> 00:23:48.510
or decreasing primary
key, you basically

00:23:48.510 --> 00:23:51.750
always end up in one
split if you basically

00:23:51.750 --> 00:23:53.790
append to this table.

00:23:53.790 --> 00:23:56.340
So you are hot spotting
on that one split,

00:23:56.340 --> 00:23:59.740
and that restricts
your scalability.

00:23:59.740 --> 00:24:02.840
Now basically the way
that we get write and read

00:24:02.840 --> 00:24:06.190
scalability is by
moving around the splits

00:24:06.190 --> 00:24:09.050
that I mentioned earlier
to different compute nodes.

00:24:09.050 --> 00:24:12.290
So if we see one of the
compute nodes is very busy,

00:24:12.290 --> 00:24:15.220
we basically take away that
split and give it another one.

00:24:15.220 --> 00:24:17.530
And by that, we have
to scalability in reads

00:24:17.530 --> 00:24:18.610
and writes.

00:24:18.610 --> 00:24:21.460
Now if you use monotonic
increasing primary keys,

00:24:21.460 --> 00:24:24.220
and that always goes
through the same split,

00:24:24.220 --> 00:24:26.450
you're basically
hot-spotting on a part,

00:24:26.450 --> 00:24:30.230
which we cannot
distribute any further.

00:24:30.230 --> 00:24:33.420
So also don't use timestamp
for this, for instance.

00:24:33.420 --> 00:24:35.080
Another thing is
where you also want

00:24:35.080 --> 00:24:36.910
to be careful as
I just mentioned

00:24:36.910 --> 00:24:39.170
the technique of interleaving.

00:24:39.170 --> 00:24:43.530
But use it in a way that
you don't over-interleave,

00:24:43.530 --> 00:24:45.650
because this also means--

00:24:45.650 --> 00:24:47.620
data co-location
means you can also

00:24:47.620 --> 00:24:50.110
hotspot on certain
things in your tables

00:24:50.110 --> 00:24:53.090
if you overuse interleaving.

00:24:53.090 --> 00:24:56.820
So here's an example of
what I just talked about.

00:24:56.820 --> 00:25:00.270
So if you have for instance,
here a timestamp that we use,

00:25:00.270 --> 00:25:04.890
and if we insert always with a
timestamp as our primary key,

00:25:04.890 --> 00:25:10.340
it all goes to one split.

00:25:10.340 --> 00:25:12.470
Now how can I avoid this?

00:25:12.470 --> 00:25:15.890
So one good practice to
avoid this is using UUIDs.

00:25:15.890 --> 00:25:20.630
So we recommend using UUIDs
version 4 as primary keys.

00:25:20.630 --> 00:25:23.450
Now there might be certain
situations where you cannot do

00:25:23.450 --> 00:25:24.710
this.

00:25:24.710 --> 00:25:28.370
So what you also can do is you
can use a sharding key in front

00:25:28.370 --> 00:25:32.000
of your timestamp, if you need
to use timestamp, for instance.

00:25:32.000 --> 00:25:34.250
Or monotonically
increasing numbers,

00:25:34.250 --> 00:25:37.220
you can basically use a
sharding key in front of it

00:25:37.220 --> 00:25:39.110
to accommodate that.

00:25:39.110 --> 00:25:42.140
Another thing what you want
to do if you move to Spanner

00:25:42.140 --> 00:25:46.370
is really designing your schema
on your primary workloads,

00:25:46.370 --> 00:25:49.710
so that your primary
workload performs best.

00:25:49.710 --> 00:25:53.730
So here's an example
of how we basically

00:25:53.730 --> 00:25:56.950
prefixed our primary
key with a sharding ID.

00:25:56.950 --> 00:26:01.890
And by that, we can split up the
inserts into multiple splits,

00:26:01.890 --> 00:26:03.910
and we can move
these splits around

00:26:03.910 --> 00:26:08.930
to different compute nodes
to have the scalability.

00:26:08.930 --> 00:26:11.750
Now we switch to
the demo computer.

00:26:14.740 --> 00:26:17.170
To get started
with Cloud Spanner,

00:26:17.170 --> 00:26:22.000
your first stop is
cloud.google.com/spanner.

00:26:22.000 --> 00:26:26.660
And this gives you all the
information and high level

00:26:26.660 --> 00:26:29.440
of what Spanner is, but also
all the pointers to client

00:26:29.440 --> 00:26:31.280
libraries and things like that.

00:26:31.280 --> 00:26:34.390
What I want to point out here
is on the bottom of this entry

00:26:34.390 --> 00:26:37.560
page, you see things
like a comparison

00:26:37.560 --> 00:26:42.810
of how does cap, for
instance, play into Spanner?

00:26:42.810 --> 00:26:44.790
Does it defeat it or not?

00:26:44.790 --> 00:26:45.730
Things like that.

00:26:45.730 --> 00:26:48.570
So [INAUDIBLE] took
a take on explaining

00:26:48.570 --> 00:26:53.550
Spanner and the [INAUDIBLE] and
how TrueTime plays into that.

00:26:53.550 --> 00:26:56.670
Also what are really nice
and really great material

00:26:56.670 --> 00:27:00.660
is, if you come from a sharded
MySQL or sharded PostgreSQL,

00:27:00.660 --> 00:27:03.270
we have Cloud Spanner case
studies which explain to you

00:27:03.270 --> 00:27:07.230
how you can do this transition
from such a system to Spanner,

00:27:07.230 --> 00:27:09.930
and how you have to take
care of certain things.

00:27:09.930 --> 00:27:13.860
So [INAUDIBLE],, one of
our early access partners,

00:27:13.860 --> 00:27:18.240
basically customers did the
transition for a sharded MySQL

00:27:18.240 --> 00:27:20.640
to Spanner, and they
explain in detail

00:27:20.640 --> 00:27:24.090
what kind of considerations
they had to do.

00:27:24.090 --> 00:27:26.550
Now for the
remainder of my demo,

00:27:26.550 --> 00:27:30.930
I want to go back to the schema
that you saw earlier today

00:27:30.930 --> 00:27:32.580
in the keynote.

00:27:32.580 --> 00:27:34.500
So what we had
there is a scenario

00:27:34.500 --> 00:27:36.090
of a ticket sales system.

00:27:36.090 --> 00:27:38.340
And this ticket
sales system, it's

00:27:38.340 --> 00:27:41.930
really important that we sell
each ticket exactly once.

00:27:41.930 --> 00:27:46.030
So that's why we choose to
use Spanner for that use case.

00:27:46.030 --> 00:27:50.130
Now we wanted to have it
is somewhat realistic.

00:27:50.130 --> 00:27:52.710
So actually we came
up with some schema

00:27:52.710 --> 00:27:55.350
to make it somewhat realistic.

00:27:55.350 --> 00:27:58.730
So what you can see here is
it all revolves around events.

00:27:58.730 --> 00:28:01.370
So you have multiple
events which

00:28:01.370 --> 00:28:03.030
you can categorize
in a multi-event,

00:28:03.030 --> 00:28:07.990
like a tour of a singer or
a star or things like that.

00:28:07.990 --> 00:28:10.050
And then we have venues.

00:28:10.050 --> 00:28:12.240
These venues have different
seating categories

00:28:12.240 --> 00:28:15.750
like general admission, or
VIP, or premium and things

00:28:15.750 --> 00:28:16.830
like that.

00:28:16.830 --> 00:28:19.080
Now these the
seating categories,

00:28:19.080 --> 00:28:21.990
we actually choose to
interleave directly in the menu

00:28:21.990 --> 00:28:26.470
because we have to fetch
them very often together.

00:28:26.470 --> 00:28:29.280
And then we have different
prices for our events.

00:28:29.280 --> 00:28:32.070
So we actually do
connect ticket categories

00:28:32.070 --> 00:28:34.890
to seating categories, and
then have all our tickets

00:28:34.890 --> 00:28:35.610
in our database.

00:28:35.610 --> 00:28:38.460
So in this database that we
showed in the keynote today,

00:28:38.460 --> 00:28:42.540
we had up to 1 billion tickets
actually in the database

00:28:42.540 --> 00:28:44.820
that you can sell.

00:28:44.820 --> 00:28:48.930
Now while this
database is running,

00:28:48.930 --> 00:28:52.300
I can actually do things
like running queries.

00:28:52.300 --> 00:28:58.080
So in this case, I
have a query which

00:28:58.080 --> 00:29:05.290
looks at the seats available
for events that are going on

00:29:05.290 --> 00:29:08.080
right now till midnight today.

00:29:08.080 --> 00:29:10.420
So you see down here
in my where clause, I'm

00:29:10.420 --> 00:29:15.760
looking at what's happening
right now till midnight,

00:29:15.760 --> 00:29:17.560
or actually 2 AM?

00:29:17.560 --> 00:29:22.700
And I look for all the events
that are going on in Spain.

00:29:22.700 --> 00:29:27.640
So now when I run this, I will
see that this will actually

00:29:27.640 --> 00:29:28.660
take a while.

00:29:28.660 --> 00:29:33.410
And I might look at it and
say, OK, this is not actually

00:29:33.410 --> 00:29:37.160
for a transactional system where
I need to do this check really

00:29:37.160 --> 00:29:41.000
quickly, eight seconds is
definitely too long to do this.

00:29:41.000 --> 00:29:44.450
Now what you can do with
Spanner is actually directly

00:29:44.450 --> 00:29:48.240
in our cloud console, you
can inspect your queries.

00:29:48.240 --> 00:29:50.690
And you can check what
is the career plan?

00:29:50.690 --> 00:29:53.990
What you're basically used to
from any other major database

00:29:53.990 --> 00:29:56.300
where you can actually
see what is the database

00:29:56.300 --> 00:29:58.220
doing behind the scenes?

00:29:58.220 --> 00:30:01.220
And as we can see
here, we are actually

00:30:01.220 --> 00:30:03.230
scanning a lot of rows.

00:30:03.230 --> 00:30:06.300
And scanning a lot of
rows is pretty expensive.

00:30:06.300 --> 00:30:10.040
So what you can do as
in the major database

00:30:10.040 --> 00:30:15.010
systems is to actually give the
optimizer, degree optimizer,

00:30:15.010 --> 00:30:17.130
some hints on what to do.

00:30:17.130 --> 00:30:23.970
So in this case, I have an
index defined on my event table

00:30:23.970 --> 00:30:28.560
which will help me
to boil down the data

00:30:28.560 --> 00:30:29.700
that I'm actually reading.

00:30:29.700 --> 00:30:35.110
So in this case, that's one
of the extensions where I say,

00:30:35.110 --> 00:30:38.550
OK, I want to force the
index use event by day.

00:30:38.550 --> 00:30:40.260
And you see right
now it's coming back

00:30:40.260 --> 00:30:42.930
in much faster in 1.4 seconds.

00:30:42.930 --> 00:30:44.610
And if we look at
the explanation,

00:30:44.610 --> 00:30:47.530
you'll see that the rows
scanned boils down a lot.

00:30:47.530 --> 00:30:49.420
So you have the
possibility to also tweak

00:30:49.420 --> 00:30:52.740
your queries in that way.

00:30:52.740 --> 00:30:55.350
Now I have a play database.

00:30:55.350 --> 00:30:58.170
And I want to show you
really quickly how you

00:30:58.170 --> 00:31:00.660
can get started for
instance, with Java

00:31:00.660 --> 00:31:04.950
and running your own queries
with Java and Spanner.

00:31:04.950 --> 00:31:08.880
So if you set up your
environment, the first thing

00:31:08.880 --> 00:31:12.480
that you need, you need
to basically authenticate

00:31:12.480 --> 00:31:14.670
against Spanner.

00:31:14.670 --> 00:31:17.880
And if you have G Cloud
installed on your machine,

00:31:17.880 --> 00:31:20.520
or if you run your
workload on GCE,

00:31:20.520 --> 00:31:23.400
you have something called
application default

00:31:23.400 --> 00:31:24.660
credentials.

00:31:24.660 --> 00:31:27.443
And so you see here, I just
go to googlecredentials.get

00:31:27.443 --> 00:31:29.490
application default
and basically it

00:31:29.490 --> 00:31:32.440
uses my contacts that I'm
running this application in

00:31:32.440 --> 00:31:34.390
and does all the
authentication for me.

00:31:34.390 --> 00:31:36.050
So that's pretty quick.

00:31:36.050 --> 00:31:37.850
The next thing that
I want to do is

00:31:37.850 --> 00:31:43.670
I need to define which
instance do I want to talk to?

00:31:43.670 --> 00:31:45.530
And which database
do I want to talk to?

00:31:45.530 --> 00:31:47.825
So very standard,
the [INAUDIBLE]

00:31:47.825 --> 00:31:51.020
is taken from the application
default credentials,

00:31:51.020 --> 00:31:52.850
so I don't have to
define that here.

00:31:52.850 --> 00:32:00.100
So I only define the instance
name and the database name.

00:32:00.100 --> 00:32:02.700
The next thing that I
need is I basically need

00:32:02.700 --> 00:32:05.336
to start up my database client.

00:32:05.336 --> 00:32:06.960
So in this case, I
would just basically

00:32:06.960 --> 00:32:08.790
get my database with
all the information

00:32:08.790 --> 00:32:11.190
that I just mentioned,
like project ID, instance

00:32:11.190 --> 00:32:12.460
ID, and database ID.

00:32:12.460 --> 00:32:16.046
And then I can basically
start running transactions.

00:32:16.046 --> 00:32:17.670
So the first thing
that I'm going to do

00:32:17.670 --> 00:32:22.010
is I going to show you
a read-only transaction.

00:32:22.010 --> 00:32:27.440
So in this case, I basically
get from my database client,

00:32:27.440 --> 00:32:31.070
and say, OK, I just want to
do one run off of a query.

00:32:31.070 --> 00:32:33.109
So I get a single use
read-only transaction.

00:32:33.109 --> 00:32:34.900
So this doesn't keep
this transaction open.

00:32:34.900 --> 00:32:36.800
It basically gives
you the possibility

00:32:36.800 --> 00:32:38.780
to run one query,
get the results back.

00:32:38.780 --> 00:32:41.200
And that transaction is over.

00:32:41.200 --> 00:32:43.400
We are using in this
case, a read-only action

00:32:43.400 --> 00:32:46.710
because I don't want to acquire
any logs on Spanner, as I

00:32:46.710 --> 00:32:48.360
mentioned before.

00:32:48.360 --> 00:32:50.600
Now we basically do our query.

00:32:50.600 --> 00:32:53.420
In this case, I'm just
getting all events that

00:32:53.420 --> 00:32:56.380
are happening right now till--

00:33:00.410 --> 00:33:06.760
basically 10 events which
are open for sale right now.

00:33:06.760 --> 00:33:10.930
And I need to, of course,
go through my result set.

00:33:10.930 --> 00:33:14.230
So results, and,
like as you are used

00:33:14.230 --> 00:33:18.495
to from iteratable
collections, you

00:33:18.495 --> 00:33:20.620
basically you can just
iterate through your results

00:33:20.620 --> 00:33:24.360
set, and print out the results.

00:33:24.360 --> 00:33:26.960
So in this case, as you can
see in my select statement,

00:33:26.960 --> 00:33:30.050
I select the multi-event
ID and the name.

00:33:30.050 --> 00:33:31.280
So I just get string.

00:33:31.280 --> 00:33:34.580
And you can choose
either the column name,

00:33:34.580 --> 00:33:36.470
the column in your SQL query.

00:33:36.470 --> 00:33:40.170
Or you can choose
actually the column index.

00:33:40.170 --> 00:33:43.560
So in my case, I'm
choosing the column index,

00:33:43.560 --> 00:33:47.090
and then I'm running it,
and, fingers crossed,

00:33:47.090 --> 00:33:50.720
if I'm successful, it it will
come back and basically-- oh.

00:33:50.720 --> 00:33:52.220
Why is this?

00:33:52.220 --> 00:33:53.000
That was not--

00:33:58.450 --> 00:33:59.432
All right.

00:33:59.432 --> 00:34:00.414
Demo fail.

00:34:03.851 --> 00:34:10.870
[INAUDIBLE] Of course.

00:34:10.870 --> 00:34:13.150
All right.

00:34:13.150 --> 00:34:15.699
So here, in this case, basically
I'm executing the read.

00:34:15.699 --> 00:34:18.310
I'm getting my
multi-event IDs back.

00:34:18.310 --> 00:34:19.850
And I get my [INAUDIBLE].

00:34:19.850 --> 00:34:21.250
So, nice.

00:34:21.250 --> 00:34:23.050
The next thing that
I want to do is

00:34:23.050 --> 00:34:25.750
I want to do a
read-write section.

00:34:25.750 --> 00:34:28.389
Now we have the exact same
thing that I just showed--

00:34:28.389 --> 00:34:30.940
so I'm not going to
type all this again--

00:34:30.940 --> 00:34:33.940
up to the point that we
basically get the client.

00:34:33.940 --> 00:34:36.409
And then you see something
here called mutations.

00:34:36.409 --> 00:34:37.940
So what are mutations?

00:34:37.940 --> 00:34:40.520
The construct in
Spanner is everything

00:34:40.520 --> 00:34:44.139
which basically changes
data is called a mutation.

00:34:44.139 --> 00:34:46.570
We have a couple of
restrictions on mutations.

00:34:46.570 --> 00:34:48.889
So one thing is
your transactions

00:34:48.889 --> 00:34:51.050
shouldn't be bigger
than 10 megabytes.

00:34:51.050 --> 00:34:56.860
So the sweet part is about
one megabyte per transaction.

00:34:56.860 --> 00:35:02.680
Another thing is you shouldn't
change more than 20,000 cells

00:35:02.680 --> 00:35:04.930
in your transaction.

00:35:04.930 --> 00:35:06.550
What does that mean, a cell?

00:35:06.550 --> 00:35:08.465
Now we have a
table for instance,

00:35:08.465 --> 00:35:12.410
with five columns,
five attributes.

00:35:12.410 --> 00:35:16.010
Now, if you change a table, if
you have no indexes defined,

00:35:16.010 --> 00:35:19.600
and you insert a row in
this table of five columns,

00:35:19.600 --> 00:35:22.130
you end up with five mutations.

00:35:22.130 --> 00:35:26.170
Now, if you have an index
defined on your table, which

00:35:26.170 --> 00:35:30.580
basically indices two
columns of your table,

00:35:30.580 --> 00:35:32.660
then you have another
two mutations.

00:35:32.660 --> 00:35:35.410
So if you insert a row, and you
have a table with five columns,

00:35:35.410 --> 00:35:37.810
and an index with which
index two columns,

00:35:37.810 --> 00:35:39.590
you end up with seven mutations.

00:35:39.590 --> 00:35:42.850
Now the limitation is to not
have more than 20,000 mutations

00:35:42.850 --> 00:35:45.540
within one transaction.

00:35:45.540 --> 00:35:50.040
Now what I'm doing here is
basically I'm getting a UUID.

00:35:50.040 --> 00:35:53.040
I'm setting a name, country
code, email address,

00:35:53.040 --> 00:35:55.170
and adding--

00:35:55.170 --> 00:35:57.360
in this case, I'm not
having any tickets,

00:35:57.360 --> 00:35:58.980
so I'm adding null there.

00:35:58.980 --> 00:36:01.020
And I'm just running this.

00:36:01.020 --> 00:36:05.890
And that basically
inserts an account for me.

00:36:05.890 --> 00:36:08.050
So as you can see here,
if I go in preview,

00:36:08.050 --> 00:36:12.720
I have in this case, three
accounts added to my database.

00:36:12.720 --> 00:36:16.720
Now if I wanted to
an update-- this

00:36:16.720 --> 00:36:17.940
was a write-only transaction.

00:36:17.940 --> 00:36:19.690
Now if I want to do
an update, I basically

00:36:19.690 --> 00:36:21.580
do a read-write transaction.

00:36:21.580 --> 00:36:23.950
And in this case, what
I am starting with,

00:36:23.950 --> 00:36:28.210
what I'm getting is I'm using
a so-called transaction runner.

00:36:28.210 --> 00:36:29.930
And this transaction
runner, which

00:36:29.930 --> 00:36:33.670
you give it a basically
a runnable, where

00:36:33.670 --> 00:36:35.600
you abstract array.

00:36:35.600 --> 00:36:37.700
You give it a basic
function to execute.

00:36:37.700 --> 00:36:39.160
Why are we doing this?

00:36:39.160 --> 00:36:42.110
The reason for this is you
basically give the client

00:36:42.110 --> 00:36:43.810
a function to execute.

00:36:43.810 --> 00:36:46.240
And if the client will
by itself do the redo

00:36:46.240 --> 00:36:48.040
if the transaction fails.

00:36:48.040 --> 00:36:50.620
So you basically give
that to our client.

00:36:50.620 --> 00:36:54.220
And you don't have to care
about redo and rerunning

00:36:54.220 --> 00:36:56.830
your transactions in
case that they fail.

00:36:56.830 --> 00:37:00.110
In this case, I'm basically
reading all the accounts

00:37:00.110 --> 00:37:07.000
with my email address,
and I'm updating my name

00:37:07.000 --> 00:37:08.240
on these accounts.

00:37:08.240 --> 00:37:10.660
So once this is run,
as you can see here,

00:37:10.660 --> 00:37:13.330
I can go back to
my preview, and you

00:37:13.330 --> 00:37:19.450
will see that the name has
changed in all three rows.

00:37:19.450 --> 00:37:21.460
Now last but not least,
what I want to show

00:37:21.460 --> 00:37:26.850
is I want to clean up
as well, of course.

00:37:26.850 --> 00:37:32.180
And the way that you can select
data with the client library

00:37:32.180 --> 00:37:34.520
is not only per SQL.

00:37:34.520 --> 00:37:36.789
You can also use the read APIs.

00:37:36.789 --> 00:37:38.330
And for instance,
here, in this case,

00:37:38.330 --> 00:37:40.130
I want to delete
all the data that I

00:37:40.130 --> 00:37:41.350
have in my account table.

00:37:41.350 --> 00:37:44.990
So I use the keyset
all, where I should not

00:37:44.990 --> 00:37:46.770
use an SQL statement.

00:37:46.770 --> 00:37:48.860
I actually use a
mutation where I

00:37:48.860 --> 00:37:53.150
say I want to have just
everything in this table,

00:37:53.150 --> 00:37:57.090
based on the keyset all,
and just delete this.

00:37:57.090 --> 00:37:59.380
And once we do
this, you will see--

00:37:59.380 --> 00:38:01.560
so basically you can
choose between SQL

00:38:01.560 --> 00:38:05.400
and our read APIs to
work with Spanner.

00:38:05.400 --> 00:38:07.170
And so we go back
and we go here,

00:38:07.170 --> 00:38:09.010
we will see that all
the data is being gone.

00:38:11.790 --> 00:38:12.290
All right.

00:38:12.290 --> 00:38:21.110
Going back to the slides,
so how does Spanner

00:38:21.110 --> 00:38:23.090
compare to all
the other products

00:38:23.090 --> 00:38:24.830
that you might have
heard about today

00:38:24.830 --> 00:38:28.160
or you might hear in the
future or did you hear about

00:38:28.160 --> 00:38:29.720
in the past?

00:38:29.720 --> 00:38:31.940
So there's a multitude
of different kinds

00:38:31.940 --> 00:38:35.450
of storage and database
products under GCP.

00:38:35.450 --> 00:38:38.900
So if you look at more
in-memory database,

00:38:38.900 --> 00:38:41.540
and you're writing your
applications in App Engine,

00:38:41.540 --> 00:38:45.090
you can use App Engine
Memcache, for instance.

00:38:45.090 --> 00:38:46.790
Now, if you have
something where you

00:38:46.790 --> 00:38:48.660
don't need the
high availability,

00:38:48.660 --> 00:38:50.860
the horizontal
scalability for write

00:38:50.860 --> 00:38:54.890
and read, and the
database which can

00:38:54.890 --> 00:38:57.510
grow across terabytes of data.

00:38:57.510 --> 00:39:00.020
So our example demo
database that we

00:39:00.020 --> 00:39:02.810
had had 30 terabytes of data.

00:39:02.810 --> 00:39:06.170
If you look at it like a
traditional vertical scalable

00:39:06.170 --> 00:39:08.090
database system,
you basically end up

00:39:08.090 --> 00:39:10.550
with a couple of terabytes
that you can scale to,

00:39:10.550 --> 00:39:12.630
and then it gets
really difficult.

00:39:12.630 --> 00:39:15.100
So if you don't
have a lot of data,

00:39:15.100 --> 00:39:20.030
if you don't need a
high available system,

00:39:20.030 --> 00:39:24.410
and if you want to run
applications that are already

00:39:24.410 --> 00:39:27.530
well integrated with things
like MySQL or PostgreSQL,

00:39:27.530 --> 00:39:31.100
then you can use our managed
Cloud SQL service, which

00:39:31.100 --> 00:39:35.060
provides you MySQL or PostgreSQL
as a managed solution,

00:39:35.060 --> 00:39:39.050
with the possibility to do
read replicas and failovers,

00:39:39.050 --> 00:39:40.340
and things like that.

00:39:40.340 --> 00:39:42.560
Now if you need the things
that I just mentioned--

00:39:42.560 --> 00:39:44.510
horizontal scalability,
high availability,

00:39:44.510 --> 00:39:45.570
things like that--

00:39:45.570 --> 00:39:48.530
then you want to
start with Spanner.

00:39:48.530 --> 00:39:51.380
Now if you don't need the
relational data model,

00:39:51.380 --> 00:39:53.870
if you want a more
NoSQL data model,

00:39:53.870 --> 00:39:56.100
and you don't need the
transactional consistency

00:39:56.100 --> 00:40:00.710
and [INAUDIBLE] semantics
that Spanner and Cloud SQL

00:40:00.710 --> 00:40:05.390
flavors are providing, then
we have Cloud Datastore

00:40:05.390 --> 00:40:08.960
for a more document
based hierarchical model.

00:40:08.960 --> 00:40:15.470
Or if you look at a system where
you have basically key value

00:40:15.470 --> 00:40:17.990
base, then you have
Cloud Bigtable,

00:40:17.990 --> 00:40:22.150
which provides you tremendous
write and read performance

00:40:22.150 --> 00:40:27.455
in single digit milliseconds
for really high throughput.

00:40:27.455 --> 00:40:29.690
Cloud Datastore
and Cloud BigTable

00:40:29.690 --> 00:40:33.200
are fully managed solutions
as well, which are innovated.

00:40:33.200 --> 00:40:35.420
You don't think about
machines anymore.

00:40:35.420 --> 00:40:37.580
They scale with your
needs from start

00:40:37.580 --> 00:40:43.110
to terabytes and
petabytes of data.

00:40:43.110 --> 00:40:45.840
Now if you have
non-structured data,

00:40:45.840 --> 00:40:48.420
you're looking at cloud
storage for instance.

00:40:48.420 --> 00:40:51.110
And if you are looking into
a data warehouse system

00:40:51.110 --> 00:40:54.670
where you'd want to do analytics
with a more full fledged SQL

00:40:54.670 --> 00:40:58.960
support, then you look into
BigQuery, for instance.

00:40:58.960 --> 00:41:02.370
Now there are a
couple of decisions

00:41:02.370 --> 00:41:06.300
that you can make along the way,
if you are at the point where

00:41:06.300 --> 00:41:07.900
which systems should I use?

00:41:07.900 --> 00:41:11.760
It's basically a reiterations of
the slide that we saw earlier,

00:41:11.760 --> 00:41:13.320
where you basically
can ask yourself

00:41:13.320 --> 00:41:16.590
a couple of questions in terms
of, is your data structured?

00:41:16.590 --> 00:41:18.630
If not, you can go
to cloud storage.

00:41:18.630 --> 00:41:21.780
If you need a mobile SDK,
you can go to Firebase

00:41:21.780 --> 00:41:26.820
and use Firebase Storage to have
all the integrations with IoS

00:41:26.820 --> 00:41:30.300
as Android and a
lot of the SDKs.

00:41:30.300 --> 00:41:32.730
Now if your data is
actually structured

00:41:32.730 --> 00:41:36.120
and you want to do
analytics on top of it,

00:41:36.120 --> 00:41:38.970
then you're looking
more to the things

00:41:38.970 --> 00:41:41.340
like Cloud BigTable
and BigQuery.

00:41:41.340 --> 00:41:45.480
If your primary workload
it is non-analytical,

00:41:45.480 --> 00:41:47.520
then you make the decision
between relational

00:41:47.520 --> 00:41:50.520
versus non-relational, where
in the relational case,

00:41:50.520 --> 00:41:54.380
you go to Cloud SQL or Spanner,
and the non-relational case

00:41:54.380 --> 00:41:58.410
you go to Cloud
Datastore or Firebase DB.

00:41:58.410 --> 00:42:00.930
And with that, I'm
done with my talk.

00:42:00.930 --> 00:42:03.150
And thank you so much
for your attention.

00:42:03.150 --> 00:42:05.250
[APPLAUSE]

00:42:06.450 --> 00:42:09.800
[MUSIC PLAYING]

